WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.021 --> 00:00:00.805
This is Foehn:

2
00:00:00.855 --> 00:00:04.019
a forecast of air pollution,
one kilometre at a time,

3
00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:06.402
for every inhabited place on Earth.

4
00:00:11.054 --> 00:00:12.264
Let's take one question.

5
00:00:12.354 --> 00:00:15.744
What will the air over Delhi
contain, five days from now?

6
00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:17.338
Start with the weather forecast:

7
00:00:17.408 --> 00:00:19.298
the wind that will carry the air there.

8
00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:21.457
Now, let's play it in reverse.

9
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:24.877
A physics engine called
FLEXGREEN follows the

10
00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:27.757
air that will arrive
in Delhi back in time,

11
00:00:27.807 --> 00:00:29.377
to where it was five days earlier.

12
00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:32.597
Particles of air are followed
through the three-dimensional wind.

13
00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:34.557
The blue ones stay near the ground;

14
00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:36.337
the pink ones travel aloft.

15
00:00:36.521 --> 00:00:38.471
The same is done for every place on land.

16
00:00:38.581 --> 00:00:39.931
And none of this is learned:

17
00:00:39.981 --> 00:00:40.831
it is computed.

18
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:44.117
How does FLEXGREEN know where the air was?

19
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.717
Take the air above one
place on the forecast day.

20
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.837
Where was it the day before,
and the days before that?

21
00:00:49.554 --> 00:00:53.164
FLEXGREEN takes the weather
forecast and runs it backwards.

22
00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:56.684
Every fifteen minutes, it moves a
particle of air against the wind,

23
00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:57.804
in three dimensions,

24
00:00:57.854 --> 00:01:00.664
with a small random kick for
the turbulence near the ground.

25
00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:04.858
It does this for thirty-two
particles over every place on land,

26
00:01:04.908 --> 00:01:07.338
released at random moments of the day.

27
00:01:07.388 --> 00:01:10.238
Together, they draw
where that air came from.

28
00:01:10.954 --> 00:01:13.124
On the way, each particle fades:

29
00:01:13.254 --> 00:01:16.304
rain and the ground take
part of its aerosol away.

30
00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:18.984
What remains is its weight.

31
00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:23.231
Where the particles were, and their
weights, make a map for each place:

32
00:01:23.841 --> 00:01:27.071
its Green's function, named after
the mathematician George Green.

33
00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:31.151
Multiply the start day by this map,
and you get what the air brings there.

34
00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:35.051
The same map works for
pollution, smoke and emissions:

35
00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:40.091
computed once, with no learned
parameter, in about five minutes a day.

36
00:01:41.921 --> 00:01:44.751
Now, look at what that air
was carrying on the start day.

37
00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:47.317
Each particle reads the
pollution where it was.

38
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.010
We keep the air that
started near the ground,

39
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.050
weight each reading by how much
of that air survived the journey,

40
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:54.800
and average.

41
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.210
Carried forward to Delhi,

42
00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.710
that air brings about eighty-four
micrograms per cubic metre.

43
00:02:00.221 --> 00:02:02.351
Done for every place, this gives a map:

44
00:02:02.401 --> 00:02:04.451
one of forty-four transport maps.

45
00:02:04.941 --> 00:02:07.351
Thirty-two others are
made from tracers that

46
00:02:07.401 --> 00:02:09.371
the network learns from the start day;

47
00:02:09.461 --> 00:02:14.051
the rest are computed, such as the
emissions the air takes up on its way.

48
00:02:14.421 --> 00:02:16.231
Here is one of those learned tracers.

49
00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:18.591
The encoder makes it from the start day;

50
00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:20.351
nothing tells it what it should mean,

51
00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:22.271
it is learned together with the forecast.

52
00:02:22.581 --> 00:02:25.211
The same transport then
reads it where the air was.

53
00:02:25.361 --> 00:02:27.031
That is what the generator receives.

54
00:02:27.555 --> 00:02:28.325
Now, the generator.

55
00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:29.905
Take a single pixel: Delhi.

56
00:02:30.335 --> 00:02:33.425
The forecast does not start
from a guess, but from noise.

57
00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:36.905
Sixty-four random numbers, one
for each member of the ensemble.

58
00:02:37.388 --> 00:02:37.998
At each step,

59
00:02:38.048 --> 00:02:41.358
a neural network looks at the
current state and at the conditions,

60
00:02:41.408 --> 00:02:43.498
and tells it which way to move.

61
00:02:43.821 --> 00:02:46.151
In four steps, the noise
becomes a forecast.

62
00:02:51.821 --> 00:02:55.731
Sixty-four noise draws become
sixty-four forecasts: an ensemble.

63
00:02:55.781 --> 00:02:58.771
And the estimate made afterwards
falls right inside it.

64
00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:01.251
Now do the same for a
whole continent at once:

65
00:03:01.301 --> 00:03:04.491
twenty-nine million pixels, in
a single pass of the network.

66
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.530
Watch the noise fade, as the
kilometre-scale detail appears.

67
00:03:14.554 --> 00:03:15.584
So here is the whole machine.

68
00:03:15.974 --> 00:03:18.144
Computed transport tells
where the air came from.

69
00:03:18.194 --> 00:03:19.884
Learned tracers say what it carried.

70
00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:23.044
And the generator turns noise
into a one-kilometre forecast.

71
00:03:23.488 --> 00:03:27.183
Every day ahead, every member,
all land at one kilometre.

72
00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:28.323
This is Foehn.

